# Tooling

<!-- Drop-in block: merge into your repo's AGENTS.md (have CLAUDE.md import it
     with a line reading @AGENTS.md, or symlink, so there is one source of
     truth across agents). These lines load at every session start and are
     re-sent on every turn - keep only what survives the test "would removing
     this cause the agent a mistake?", and keep the whole file under ~200
     lines. This block is the always-on layer; the per-change capability
     snapshot and per-task tool pointers live in the OpenSpec artifacts, which
     load just-in-time. -->

- Prefer a real capability over its approximation: the database MCP over
  hand-written SQL against a guessed schema, browser automation over
  reasoning about how a page renders, the design-tool MCP's tokens over
  eyeballed values, an installed skill over memory of its domain.
- Subagents inherit nothing from this session. A dispatch prompt must name
  the tools, skills, and file paths the subagent needs - an unmentioned
  capability is an unused one.
- Read ./TOOLS.md before starting work in this repo, and again before
  changing dependencies, scripts, build config, test setup, or deploy
  settings. It is the canonical, evidence-cited map of this project's
  stack, commands, and committed agent capabilities. If it disagrees with
  the manifest, lockfile, or config, the repo wins - verify, then correct
  TOOLS.md as part of your change. (No TOOLS.md yet? The
  mapping-project-tooling skill creates one from repo evidence.)
- OpenSpec changes record a fresh per-change capability snapshot in the
  first artifact (`brainstorm.md` or `design-brief.md`, section "Relevant
  Capabilities") and per-task pointers in `tasks.md` Tooling lines. For
  change work, trust those over this list - they are newer.
- Process skills outrank role skills. Inside an OpenSpec change, the
  skills the schema names are the discipline (superpowers for TDD, review,
  debugging, verification; impeccable and the confirmed brief for design);
  role/tool skill packs (e.g. engineering-team collections) contribute
  scripts, personas, and patterns through Tooling lines. When a role
  skill's workflow disagrees with the named discipline - generating tests
  from written code instead of writing the failing test first, scaffold
  styling instead of the committed visual world - the discipline wins and
  the role skill supplies tools only.
